Abstract 摘要

Adaptive cyber-physical systems increasingly rely on continuous interaction with human users, creating conditions under which identity-relevant representations may persist beyond intended session boundaries.

This paper identifies a previously unformalized structural risk, termed Persistent Identity Modeling (PIM), in which adaptive systems implicitly encode and accumulate identity-specific information across sessions without explicit authorization or control.

To address this risk, we propose NOIRÉA, an architectural framework for session-bounded identity governance. NOIRÉA formalizes system representations as a decomposition of task-specific and identity-relevant components, and introduces enforceable requirements for identity isolation, controlled persistence, bounded influence, and revocable reset.

The framework operationalizes these constraints through explicit boundary insertion points across feature extraction, model representation, and session transitions, enabling direct measurement and control of identity leakage. We instantiate identity influence using probe-based separability metrics and demonstrate that systems without boundary enforcement exhibit significant cross-session identity persistence, while NOIRÉA-constrained systems substantially reduce identity leakage.

Unlike unconstrained adaptive systems that permit implicit global representation drift, NOIRÉA restricts adaptation to explicitly governed subspaces, thereby limiting unintended identity persistence while avoiding unnecessary global recomputation overhead.

These findings position identity lifecycle governance as a first-class architectural requirement for adaptive systems, with implications for reliability, safety, and human–AI interaction integrity.

