2. Problem Formulation

Adaptive systems maintain internal representations that evolve over time through interaction with data.
Let M_t denote the system representation at time t.

We consider that system representations can be decomposed into two components:
	•	M_t^{\text{task}}: task-relevant representation
	•	M_t^{\text{identity}}: identity-relevant representation

Such that:

M_t = M_t^{\text{task}} \oplus M_t^{\text{identity}}

where \oplus denotes a conceptual decomposition, not necessarily an explicit architectural separation.

⸻

2.1 Session Boundary and Identity Persistence

We define a session as a bounded interaction interval S_i = [t_0, t_T].
A new session S_{i+1} begins at time t = 0.

In an ideal system without cross-session identity persistence:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} = 0

However, in adaptive systems, identity-relevant information may persist across sessions:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} = P\left(M_{i-1,T}^{\text{identity}}\right)

where P(\cdot) is a persistence operator.

⸻

2.2 Persistent Identity Modeling (PIM)

Persistent Identity Modeling (PIM) is defined as the condition where both of the following hold:

(1) Identity-relevant representations persist across session boundaries; and
(2) These representations exert measurable influence on system behavior.

Formally, PIM exists if:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} \neq 0
\quad \text{and} \quad
\Phi\left(M_t^{\text{identity}}\right) > \tau

where:
	•	\Phi(\cdot) is an identity influence function
	•	\tau is a predefined threshold.


