2.3 Identity Influence Measurement

We define the identity influence function \Phi as the degree to which identity-relevant representations can be extracted from system states.

In practice, \Phi can be instantiated as a probe-based separability metric, such as:
	•	Linear classifier accuracy
	•	ROC-AUC for identity classification

Thus:

\Phi(M_t^{\text{identity}}) = \text{AUC}_{\text{identity}}

A system exhibits identity leakage when identity information is separable beyond threshold \tau.

⸻

2.4 Output Deviation Induced by Identity Persistence

Let system output be defined as:

y_t = f(x_t, M_t)

where x_t is the task input.

In the absence of identity influence:

y_t = f(x_t, M_t^{\text{task}})

However, under PIM:

y_t = f(x_t, M_t^{\text{task}}, M_t^{\text{identity}})

We define identity-induced output deviation as:

\Delta y = y_t - y_t^{\text{task}}

where y_t^{\text{task}} is the output under identity-free conditions.

⸻

2.5 Observability and Attribution

Although \Delta y may be observable at the output level, it is not directly attributable to identity persistence without explicit measurement.

We define a condition of latent identity influence:

\exists \, \Delta y \neq 0
\quad \text{such that} \quad
\Phi(M_t^{\text{identity}}) > \tau

but no explicit signal indicates identity contribution in system outputs.

This implies that identity-induced effects may:
	•	appear as normal adaptation
	•	remain hidden under stable performance metrics
	•	require representation-level analysis for detection

⸻

2.6 Problem Statement

The core problem addressed in this work is:

How to enforce constraints such that identity-relevant representations do not persist or influence system behavior beyond authorized and controlled conditions.



3. NOIRÉA Architecture

To address Persistent Identity Modeling (PIM), we propose NOIRÉA, an architectural framework that enforces session-bounded identity governance through explicit constraints on representation lifecycle.

The design principle of NOIRÉA is to prevent the simultaneous satisfaction of the two conditions defining PIM:
	•	cross-session identity persistence
	•	measurable identity influence

This is achieved by introducing enforceable constraints on how identity-relevant representations are initialized, propagated, and reset.

⸻

3.1 Representation Constraint Model

We assume the representation decomposition:

M_t = M_t^{\text{task}} \oplus M_t^{\text{identity}}

NOIRÉA does not require perfect disentanglement, but enforces operational constraints on the identity component.

⸻

3.2 R1 — Session-Bounded Identity Isolation

At the beginning of each session S_i, identity-relevant representations must not persist from prior sessions unless explicitly authorized:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} = 0

This constraint directly prevents the persistence condition required for PIM.

⸻

3.3 R2 — Controlled Cross-Session Persistence

If identity persistence is required, it must be explicitly controlled and isolated:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} = P\left(M_{i-1,T}^{\text{identity}}\right)

where P(\cdot) is an authorized persistence operator satisfying:
	•	explicit activation
	•	logical separability from task representation
	•	revocability

This ensures that identity persistence does not occur implicitly.

⸻

3.4 R3 — Bounded Identity Influence

Identity influence on system behavior must remain bounded:

\Phi\left(M_t^{\text{identity}}\right) \leq \tau

where \Phi is the identity influence metric defined in Section 2.

This constraint ensures that even if identity representations exist, they do not exert excessive influence on system outputs.

⸻

3.5 R4 — Revocable Identity Reset

The system must support explicit removal of identity-relevant representations:

R(M_t) = \left(M_t^{\text{task}}, 0\right)

such that:

\| f(x_t, M_t) - f(x_t, R(M_t)) \| \leq \epsilon

where \epsilon is a tolerance threshold.

This ensures that identity information can be removed without significantly degrading task performance.

⸻

3.6 Architectural Realization (NOIRÉA Membrane)

To enforce these constraints in practice, NOIRÉA introduces boundary insertion points within the system pipeline:
	1.	Feature Boundary
	•	prevents identity leakage from raw inputs into learned representations
	2.	Model Boundary
	•	enforces separation or projection of identity-relevant components
	3.	Session Reset Boundary
	•	ensures verifiable reset of identity state across sessions

These boundaries enable operational enforcement of R1–R4 without requiring full disentanglement of representations.

⸻

3.7 Relation to PIM

The NOIRÉA constraints ensure that at least one of the defining conditions of PIM is violated:
	•	R1 / R2 → prevent uncontrolled persistence
	•	R3 → bound influence below detection threshold
	•	R4 → allow removal of identity state

Thus, the system is prevented from satisfying:

M_{i,0}^{\text{identity}} \neq 0
\quad \text{and} \quad
\Phi(M_t^{\text{identity}}) > \tau

simultaneously.







