4.1 Objective

The objective of this evaluation is to determine whether NOIRÉA effectively reduces unintended identity persistence and influence in adaptive systems.

Specifically, we evaluate:
	1.	Whether identity-relevant representations persist across sessions
	2.	Whether such representations influence system behavior
	3.	Whether NOIRÉA reduces both persistence and influence

⸻

4.2 Experimental Setup

Synthetic Adaptive System

We construct a controlled adaptive system where task-relevant and identity-relevant signals can be independently manipulated.

Each input sample is defined as:

x = x_{\text{task}} + \alpha \cdot x_{\text{identity}}

where:
	•	x_{\text{task}}: task-relevant component
	•	x_{\text{identity}}: identity-specific component
	•	\alpha: identity signal strength

⸻

Sessions

Data is divided into multiple sessions:
	•	Session S_i: training phase
	•	Session S_{i+1}: evaluation phase

We ensure that identity signals are not explicitly provided in the evaluation session.

⸻

Models

We compare two system configurations:
	•	Baseline (No Boundary)
	•	standard adaptive model
	•	no identity control
	•	NOIRÉA-Constrained System
	•	identity isolation at session boundary
	•	influence constraint enforced

⸻

4.3 Metrics

Identity Separability (Φ)

We measure identity influence using a probe classifier trained to predict identity labels from internal representations.

\Phi = \text{ROC-AUC}_{\text{identity}}

Interpretation:
	•	\Phi \approx 0.5: no identity leakage
	•	\Phi \gg 0.5: identity information present

⸻

Output Deviation

We measure deviation in outputs across sessions:

\Delta y = y_t - y_t^{\text{task}}

This quantifies identity-induced influence on system behavior.

⸻

4.4 Procedure
	1.	Train model on session S_i with identity signal
	2.	Transition to session S_{i+1}
	3.	Evaluate:
	•	internal representation separability
	•	output consistency under identical task inputs
	4.	Repeat across multiple random seeds

⸻

