4.6 Analysis

The baseline system exhibits clear identity persistence and influence:
	•	identity signals remain detectable across sessions
	•	outputs are affected by prior identity exposure

In contrast, NOIRÉA:
	•	reduces identity separability to near-random levels
	•	limits identity-induced output deviation

These results demonstrate that identity persistence is not merely observable, but can be structurally controlled through architectural constraints.

⸻

4.7 Discussion of Validity

This experiment isolates identity persistence in a controlled setting.

While synthetic, it provides:
	•	a proof of architectural effectiveness
	•	a measurable link between representation and behavior

Future work will evaluate NOIRÉA in real-world adaptive systems (e.g., BCI, robotics).

