🧾 Section 5 — Conclusion（可直接用）

This paper introduced Persistent Identity Modeling (PIM) as a previously unformalized structural risk in adaptive cyber-physical systems, arising from unintended persistence and influence of identity-relevant representations across session boundaries.

We proposed NOIRÉA, an architectural framework that enforces session-bounded identity governance through explicit constraints on representation decomposition, persistence, influence, and reset. By formalizing identity and task representations and introducing measurable criteria for identity influence, we demonstrated that identity persistence is not only detectable but can be systematically controlled.

Experimental results show that systems without architectural constraints exhibit significant identity leakage and output deviation, while NOIRÉA-constrained systems reduce identity separability and stabilize system behavior across sessions.

These findings suggest that identity lifecycle governance should be treated as a first-class architectural requirement in adaptive systems, particularly in domains involving continuous human interaction. Beyond privacy considerations, the results highlight a broader reliability concern: system behavior can be implicitly shaped by accumulated identity signals in ways that are not directly observable or attributable without dedicated mechanisms.

Future work will extend this framework to real-world adaptive systems and explore integration with broader governance architectures for human–AI–physical interaction.


